# Build + generated artifacts (the recompiled substrate is ~hundreds of MB of emitted C)
build/
scratch/
generated/

# The recompiled substrate's INPUTS come from YOUR OWN disc and are never committed: the disc image
# and anything extracted from it are the copyright holder's, not this repo's.
#
# Kept NARROW on purpose. Everything extracted lands under scratch/ (already ignored above); the
# patterns here are the belt to that braces, for a file dropped in the repo root. Broad patterns
# (*.exe, *.bin) would also match ordinary PROSE naming those files in docs, which floods
# tools/go_public.py's "docs referencing gitignored items" check with false positives — and a gate
# that cries wolf is one people stop reading.
*.chd
.env
# NOT listed here, deliberately: `SLUS_005.61` / `SLUS_*.*`. go_public.py's check C ("docs referencing
# SENSITIVE gitignored items") turns every gitignore pattern into a search token, so those two would
# produce the tokens `SLUS_005.61` and `SLUS_`, which every doc in this repo names in prose — the audit
# becomes a wall of false positives and a gate that cries wolf is one people stop reading. The
# extracted executable lands under `scratch/bin/megamanx4/`, ignored above, which is where the tooling
# puts it; a copy dropped loose in the repo root is caught by review and by go_public.py's check A
# (which matches PATHS, not prose, and does list SLUS_005.61). Same tradeoff vagrant's and spyro's
# .gitignore document.

# The framework. Not tracked: either a symlink to the workspace shared clone (local) or a
# private clone at psxport.pin (fresh machine). Managed by tools/psxport_sync.py.
/external/psxport
# The vendored AGPL decomp is a REFERENCE; we never build it here, but a curious session might.
# external/mmx4/disks/ matters specifically: upstream's README says the retail SLUS_005.61 goes in
# disks/us, i.e. the natural way to use that repo is to drop OUR extracted executable into it.
external/mmx4/build/
external/mmx4/disks/
external/mmx4/.venv/
external/mmx4/expected/
*.log

# Python bytecode — never committed. Compiled .pyc embed ABSOLUTE SOURCE PATHS, i.e. a machine-path
# leak that a TEXT-only audit cannot see, because they are binary.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Editor / OS noise
compile_commands.json
.DS_Store
